        "content": "{\n  \"ranking\": [\n    {\n      \"id\": \"patch_c24a658dacfc\",\n      \"failure_port\": \"drop_frame\",\n      \"source_line\": 110,\n      \"mechanism\": \"The failing trace shows drop_frame asserted at cycles 13-14 (actual=1, expected=0) during reset while a frame is being written. At line 110, the code commits wr_ptr_cur to wr_ptr unconditionally on input_axis_tlast. However, when input_axis_tuser=1, the frame should be discarded (wr_ptr_cur rolls back to wr_ptr), not committed. The patch adds the missing `!input_axis_tuser` guard so that only non-user frames advance the commit pointer, preventing spurious drop_frame activation and the subsequent output_axis_tdata corruption at cycle 16.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"patch_6dd7aead91eb\",\n      \"failure_port\": \"drop_frame\",\n      \"source_line\": 92,\n      \"mechanism\": \"At line 92, input_axis_tready is assigned `(~full | DROP_WHEN_FULL)` without gating on `!rst`. During reset (cycles 5-7 show rst=1), the FIFO advertises ready, allowing the testbench to push data that the write block (line 99) ignores because its `if (rst)` branch only resets wr_ptr. This desynchronizes wr_ptr_cur from wr_ptr, causing the drop_frame logic to misfire at cycles 13-14. Qualifying the ready signal with `!rst` blocks input during reset, keeping pointers aligned.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"patch_00699d2af945\",\n      \"failure_port\": \"drop_frame\",\n      \"source_line\": 100,\n      \"mechanism\": \"The condition `full | full_cur | drop_frame` at line 100 triggers the drop-frame state machine on any beat where the FIFO is full or already dropping. The trace shows drop_frame rising at cycles 13-14 without input_axis_tlast, meaning the state machine entered drop mode mid-frame. Adding `&& (input_axis_tlast)` restricts entry to frame boundaries, so drop_frame only asserts when a full frame cannot be accepted, matching the expected zero at cycles 13-14 and preserving output_axis_tdata at cycle 16.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"patch_2091b2021c83\",\n      \"failure_port\": \"output_axis_tdata\",\n      \"source_line\": 127,\n      \"mechanism\": \"At cycle 16 the output_axis_tdata is 1 instead of 6 and output_axis_tvalid is 0 instead of 1, indicating the read-side state was not cleared by reset. Line 127's reset block only clears rd_ptr; data_out_reg retains stale payload. The patch adds `data_out_reg <= 0` in the same reset block, ensuring the output register is clean when valid re-asserts after reset.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"patch_984eed6c8fb1\",\n      \"failure_port\": \"drop_frame\",\n      \"source_line\": 103,\n      \"mechanism\": \"Inside the drop-frame state machine (line 103), `input_axis_tlast` alone terminates dropping and attempts to commit/rollback pointers. If drop_frame is already 1, this fires on every tlast, including the cycle 13-14 region where the trace expects drop_frame=0. Adding `&& (!drop_frame)` ensures the termination logic only runs once per drop episode, preventing re-entry glitches that corrupt drop_frame and downstream output_axis_tdata.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"patch_15a85deae41e\",\n      \"failure_port\": \"drop_frame\",\n      \"source_line\": 99,\n      \"mechanism\": \"The write block (line 99) resets wr_ptr on rst but leaves wr_ptr_cur and drop_frame untouched. The trace shows rst=1 at cycles 7-8; afterwards wr_ptr_cur holds a stale offset, so the full_cur check misfires and drop_frame asserts at cycles 13-14. Inserting `wr_ptr_cur <= 0` in the reset block aligns both pointers, eliminating the false full_cur condition.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"patch_eb889265e9be\",\n      \"failure_port\": \"drop_frame\",\n      \"source_line\": 97,\n      \"mechanism\": \"The write block enters on `if (rst) ... else if (write)`. When drop_frame=1, `write` remains true (input valid and ready), so the else-if executes and re-evaluates the drop state machine, bouncing drop_frame. Guarding the rst branch with `&& (!drop_frame)` is orthogonal, but the evidence anchors show this patch correlates with the same three failures; the mechanism is that preventing reset handling while dropping avoids pointer corruption that later manifests as drop_frame=1 at cycles 13-14 and bad output_axis_tdata at cycle 16.\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"id\": \"patch_5ca8f64228ff\",\n      \"failure_port\": \"drop_frame\",\n      \"source_line\": 99,\n      \"mechanism\": \"Changing `else if (write)` to `else if ((write) && (rst))` at line 99 makes the write block execute only when rst=1, which is the opposite of the required behavior (write should be disabled during reset). The evidence anchors nevertheless match the three failure ports, indicating the static dependency over-approximation links this line to the drop_frame/output_axis_tdata mismatches, but the edit direction is likely incorrect for a real fix.\"\n    }\n  ]\n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n  \n